>
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="!mb-6 !text-sm"><a href="/research" class="!text-[var(--color-accent)]">← Current Work</a></p>
    <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--color-text-dim)] font-medium mb-2">
      Master’s thesis · in design
    </div>
    <h1>Learning epistemic modality from linguistic input</h1>
    <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)] italic !text-lg !mb-3">
      Can a learner acquire the distinctions from the <a href="/research/three-ways">semantics paper</a>
      from linguistic input alone — and, if so, which patterns in the input are doing the work?
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-dim)] !mb-8">
      The computational companion to the semantics paper. A language model is used here as an instrument
      for one question — what a linguistic distribution can support in a controlled learner — not as a
      stand-in for a developing child.
    </p>

    <p>
      My master’s thesis takes the same three-way distinction — one-sided possibility, explicit two-sided
      openness, and an explicit report of non-resolution — and asks an acquisition question: can a system
      that sees only language come to make the distinction, and what in the input is necessary for it to
      emerge? The thesis is the computational extension of the paper, not a restatement of the theory.
    </p>

    <h2>Why this follows from the semantics</h2>
    <p>
      Once the paper fixes what the distinction is and what would count as evidence for it, a learnability
      question becomes well-posed. The same criteria that separate a maintained possibility from ordinary
      uncertainty — that the effect is specific to the proposition, survives the sentence, and disappears
      when the proposition is ruled out — can be turned into a test of whether a trained model has actually
      acquired the distinction rather than a cheaper statistical shortcut. The thesis begins from that
      analysis; the formal work is the paper’s, not repeated here.
    </p>

    <h2>The planned study</h2>
    <p>The current candidate design has three steps, in order:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Audit the evidence.</strong> Find, in natural corpora, the cues that can be reliably annotated — where a possibility is kept open, invoked again later, or closed off — and measure how the evidence is distributed.</li>
      <li><strong>Intervene on the input.</strong> Train a small model under matched conditions that remove, shuffle, or enrich those cues, and ask whether the target distinction still becomes learnable, and how the learning trajectory changes.</li>
      <li><strong>Test generalization.</strong> Check whether the model extends the distinction to unseen words, templates, and contexts — with an explicit control for surface cues such as frequency, topic, length, and local co-occurrence, so that an apparent success is not a shortcut wearing the right answer’s clothes.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What this is not</h2>
    <p>
      The thesis is deliberately bounded. Its default core is a formal analysis plus one credible
      behavioural or computational experiment. Training runs from scratch, checkpoint-trajectory analysis,
      probing, and circuit-level mechanism work are not default chapters; they enter only where a specific
      question needs them. Developmental cognition is a theoretical reference that keeps the acquisition
      question honest — the thesis does not run studies with children, and does not treat the model as a
      model of a child.
    </p>

    <h2>Status</h2>
    <p class="cw-status">
      In design. The scope is intentionally not frozen: it waits on the semantics paper’s construct gate
      and on advisor feedback before the exact question and experiment are fixed. The controlled-input
      training is written above as the priority candidate, not as a commitment. No study has yet produced
      a result.
    </p>

